reims-vgpu: paint the console surface when someone reads it, not on every present.

Every ScanoutUpdate the device delivered was copied into QEMU's
DisplaySurface right away (reims_vgpu_mmio_apply_scanout ->
reims_vgpu_qemu_scanout_copy -> copy_to_bgra8 / blit_bgra_buffer, 12 MB at
1179x2556) on the main loop with the BQL held, also when nobody would look at
the surface. The phone runs `-display none` (tools/run_ios_paravirt.py): no
display listener reads it, and since reims-frame-mailbox the desktop's frames
come from the mailbox file, so the surface only serves the `screendump`
fallback, which asks the device through gfx_update before reading it
(qemu_console_co_wait_update -> console_hw_update_bh -> gfx_update), as every
display refresh does.

That copy is also the present's ack (device_scanout_copy ->
note_scanout_copy_consumed clears present_action_pending and
unpainted_presents and schedules the drain); while present_action_pending is
set, device_drain returns without draining. A first version of this patch
postponed the whole call to gfx_update and with it the
ack: that showed one frame and then a frozen UI (mailbox published=1, drain
duty 0.001, SpringBoard unresponsive).

Now the paint is split. reims_vgpu_qemu_scanout_account (device_scanout_account
-> account_bgra8) takes the copy's lock, records what copy_to_bgra8 records
for a re-show of the retained +0x188 frame (note_snapshot_shown, now shared by
both) and acks the present the same way, without copying; any other paint
(pre-boundary live mapping, capture retry, no retained frame of that size)
returns ERR_STATE untouched and C calls scanout_copy at once, as before.
reims_vgpu_qemu_scanout_reshow copies the retained frame and changes nothing.
When REIMS_VGPU_FRAME_MAILBOX names a file and no host window is requested,
the MMIO shim accounts each present on delivery, marks the surface stale and
re-shows it in gfx_update after delivering pending actions. Without a window
the retained frame is always the CPU copy (display_from_resident false).
REIMS_VGPU_EAGER_CONSOLE_PAINT=1 (tools/run_ios_paravirt.py --console-paint
eager) keeps the old paint on every present. Tests: device::tests
an_accounted_present_is_acked_like_a_painted_one_and_reshown_on_demand,
a_paint_that_is_not_a_reshow_is_declined_without_an_ack.

